{
  "_about": "Anatomically Programmed Radiography (APR) chart. Selecting a protocol sets technique (kVp/mAs), anti-scatter grid, display LUT, SID, collimated field, and receptor size/orientation for that projection. Grouped body part -> region -> projection. Typical adult DR values: distal extremities 50-65 kVp no grid at 100 cm SID; proximal limb/skull/spine/abdomen/pelvis 70-90 kVp with grid at 100 cm; chest high-kVp 110-125 kVp with grid at 180 cm. coll = collimated field [width_cm, length_cm]; det = receptor [short_cm, long_cm]; orient = portrait/landscape. Teaching approximations, not a facility chart. cm = the adult thickness (caliper, along the central ray) an entry is written for: the console measures the patient there and scales mAs at fixed kV (src/core/technique.js). Entries without it are used as written. mAs calibrated on this simulator (Oct 2026): each exam exposed at its chart technique on the shipped subjects, read with the exam VOI, and its mAs moved to the station that brings DI to 0 (size-corrected exams are calibrated for the chart thickness). AP/lateral cervical and lateral forearm/elbow are not: their central rays cross anatomy that is not the exam's (the C-spine landmark sits at C1; the CT has the arms raised, and the arm against the trunk).",
  "_reference": "Merrill's Atlas / Bontrager positioning references for SID, receptor size, and collimation; grid + kVp per body-part attenuation.",
